1
00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.000
In this lesson, you'll
learn how you can extract

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.490
and normalize content from a diverse

3
00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:11.470
range of document types so your LLM
can reference information from PDFs,

4
00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:13.960
PowerPoints, Word docs, HTML, and more.

5
00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:16.199
Alright, let's dive into the code.

6
00:00:16.899 --> 00:00:20.300
First, you'll learn about why you might
want to normalize diverse document types.

7
00:00:21.179 --> 00:00:23.399
First, documents come
in a variety of formats.

8
00:00:23.679 --> 00:00:25.899
Just think of all the
documents you might have seen

9
00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:27.449
at your organization
or at your university.

10
00:00:27.809 --> 00:00:31.050
You might have seen PDFs or Word
documents or even HTML documents,

11
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.479
and all of those documents
come in different formats.

12
00:00:34.259 --> 00:00:36.500
And when you're building
an LLM application, really,

13
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.130
you don't want to have to care
about where your documents came from.

14
00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:43.679
You want them all in a common
format so that in your LLM application,

15
00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:45.619
you can treat them in the same way.

16
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.520
And so the first step in doing
that is to break those documents

17
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.939
down into common elements
like titles and narrative text.

18
00:00:52.359 --> 00:00:53.539
There are a few benefits to this.

19
00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.539
First, it allows you to
process any document in

20
00:00:56.539 --> 00:00:58.679
the same way regardless
of the source format.

21
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.890
This will allow you to
perform operations like filtering

22
00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.979
out unwanted elements
like headers and footers.

23
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.219
If you wanted to do that for
HTML and PDF documents separately,

24
00:01:08.219 --> 00:01:12.310
you would have to write separate logic
for each document. By normalizing them,

25
00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.819
you can do that all with
the same snippet of code.

26
00:01:15.379 --> 00:01:18.310
Second, it allows you to apply
downstream operations like chunking

27
00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.239
in the same way on all the
different document types.

28
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.180
And so you don't have to
create different chunking

29
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.180
strategies on a per document type basis.

30
00:01:26.599 --> 00:01:30.120
This also has benefits in terms of
reducing processing cost. Typically,

31
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.180
the most expensive
part about pre-processing

32
00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.239
documents is
extracting the initial content.

33
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.480
Downstream operations like
chunking are typically inexpensive.

34
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.939
By normalizing all of the
content to the same format,

35
00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:43.120
you can experiment with
different chunking strategies

36
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.980
in a very computationally inexpensive way.

37
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.200
Once you've normalized the content,
typically the next step is data serialization.

38
00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:54.109
So there are a few
benefits to data serialization,

39
00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:57.640
and this allows you to reuse
your outputs later without

40
00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.760
having to pre-process the documents again.

41
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.790
In this course, you'll serialize your
data as JSON. There are other options here,

42
00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.980
but JSON is convenient
for a variety of reasons.

43
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.409
First, it's a common and
well-understood structure. Second,

44
00:02:10.409 --> 00:02:12.340
it's a standard HTTP response.

45
00:02:12.759 --> 00:02:16.669
This will come into play when you're
processing documents like PDFs and

46
00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:20.259
images that require model-based
workloads that you'll run over an API.

47
00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.599
JSON can also be used in
multiple programming languages.

48
00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.520
This is important because if you're
building an application in JavaScript,

49
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:33.389
you can pre-process your documents in
Python, serialize the outputs to JSON,

50
00:02:33.389 --> 00:02:37.849
and then read those outputs into your
JavaScript application. And finally,

51
00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:40.340
JSON can also be used
for streaming use cases.

52
00:02:41.139 --> 00:02:44.000
For instance, by storing
the documents as JSON-L.

53
00:02:44.439 --> 00:02:47.090
Here's an example of what the
serialized outputs will look

54
00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:47.889
like when you pre-process
the documents in this course.

55
00:02:50.159 --> 00:02:51.979
So now you'll learn about pre-processing
a few different specific document types.

56
00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:01.610
So this is relevant for LLMs
because in a lot of cases,

57
00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:05.800
you want to read in content from the
internet into your LLM application.

58
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.000
When you're understanding HTML
documents, typically you're looking at HTML tags.

59
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.000
So for instance, an H1 tag will
indicate that content is likely a title.

60
00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:19.900
A paragraph tag will indicate that
content is likely narrative text.

61
00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.169
In addition to using these tags,

62
00:03:22.169 --> 00:03:25.710
it's also helpful to use natural
language processing capabilities to understand

63
00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:29.900
the content of the text to better
understand the categorization of the content.

64
00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:32.849
So for example, long
content with multiple sentences

65
00:03:32.849 --> 00:03:35.930
in a paragraph tag is
likely to be narrative text,

66
00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.710
whereas short content in a
paragraph tag that's all

67
00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.479
capitalized may be more
likely to be a title.

68
00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.909
So we can use both that
structured information and unstructured

69
00:03:44.909 --> 00:03:47.360
information within the
document to categorize the elements.

70
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.919
And there's an example of what that
might look like in an actual page.

71
00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.400
And so in this example here, the
title is represented by an H1 tag.

72
00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.159
And then in the second
box under narrative text,

73
00:03:59.159 --> 00:04:01.620
that content is
represented in a paragraph tag.

74
00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:04.659
Now that you've learned
about pre-processing HTML,

75
00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.720
So first, you'll import this.

76
00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.819
This just filters out warnings that
we don't want for classroom purposes.

77
00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:24.290
Next, you'll import a few helper functions

78
00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:26.620
from the unstructured open source library.

79
00:04:27.199 --> 00:04:31.589
In this case, you'll use the
partition HTML function from the unstructured

80
00:04:31.589 --> 00:04:34.519
open source library to
pre-process the HTML document.

81
00:04:34.939 --> 00:04:37.100
Later, you'll use
partition PowerPoint as well.

82
00:04:37.579 --> 00:04:41.029
After importing these helper functions
from the unstructured open source library,

83
00:04:41.029 --> 00:04:44.790
you'll also set up what you
need to use the unstructured API.

84
00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.610
You'll use the
unstructured API in cases where we

85
00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.160
need to process PDFs or
images in this course.

86
00:04:50.459 --> 00:04:52.129
That's because these
are expensive model-based

87
00:04:52.129 --> 00:04:54.079
workloads that require a bit more setup.

88
00:04:54.899 --> 00:04:59.399
This code here allows you to set up your
credentials to use the unstructured API.

89
00:05:00.439 --> 00:05:02.959
And so we're setting up the
unstructured API client here.

90
